This amplification has a natural interpretation. A larger type pool provides more opportunities for semantically similar types to co-occur, expanding the confusion space. BCS captures this expanded space: at 80 relations, extreme high-BCS batches pack tightly clustered types that the model cannot reliably distinguish, while the same count of well-separated types preserves near-solo-evaluation accuracy.

\paragraph{Cross-model validation.}
The confusability effect replicates on Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: on FewRel, the BCS gap is $-$27.1\,pp at count${}\,=\,8$ (low-BCS 78.1\% vs.\ high-BCS 51.0\%) and $-$23.9\,pp at count${}\,=\,16$ (75.5\% vs.\ 51.6\%), reaching 75--87\% of Qwen3-32B's $-$31.2\,pp. Strikingly, high-BCS accuracy is model-invariant: both models converge to $\approx$51\% under high confusability (count${}\,=\,8$: 51.0\% vs.\ 51.0\%; count${}\,=\,16$: 51.6\% vs.\ 51.6\%), while cross-model divergence is confined to the low-BCS regime where model capacity matters. This pattern indicates that confusable type sets create a task-inherent difficulty floor independent of model scale.

